{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 posits a causal mechanism (expert high-dimensional pattern recognition vs. additive checklist gates) but never addresses the problem's critical demand: it presents zero evidence that, within a single evaluation, one could distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. By arguing for the expert's superiority, it implicitly assumes away the compliance problem for its own side yet offers no diagnostic criterion or evidentiary signal that would let an evaluator tell apart true integrative judgment from an expert's post-hoc rationalization that coincides with the verified outcome. Without that distinguishing evidence, the proposed mechanism is unfalsifiable in the exact setting the problem specifies.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 introduces a task-type partition (verification-heavy vs. synthesis-heavy) as its causal mechanism, but it never supplies the evidence-within-a-single-evaluation that the problem explicitly demands. Its countercondition (a checklist that perfectly maps the synthesis process) concedes that the verification/synthesis boundary can collapse, yet it gives no diagnostic to tell, in one run, whether a panel's correct verification-heavy outcome reflects genuine reliability or mere checklist compliance tracking the right answer. The mechanism explains which side wins by task type but is silent on the problem's distinguishing-evidence requirement.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}